## Strongest confirmed theorem

Over a characteristic-zero field `L`, for monic depressed `f` of degree `m≥2` and `g=F_n(f)` with `n≥1`, vanishing of the first `m-1` Faber tails is equivalent to the existence of a unique monic depressed `K∈L[z]` of degree `d=gcd(m,n)` with `f=K^{m/d}` and `g=K^{n/d}`. The reduced zero scheme of those tails in the affine space of depressed monic degree-`m` polynomials is the closed prime power locus `A_L^{d-1}`; for `d≥2` its weighted projectivization is `P(2,3,…,d)`, and for `d=1` the projective locus is empty. In particular this is the reduced support of the ordinary unloaded `(9,12)` eight-tail system and of the ordinary unloaded `(8,12)` seven-tail system. The tail ideal need not be reduced, and the identification with a Rees exceptional fibre is not included.

